SUPPLY CHAIN MORNING - THE WEEK AHEAD
Sunday, March 15, 2026

What we are watching this week.

Monday: February retail sales. Inventory-to-sales is the derived number we care about; a strong sales print with flat inventories means restocking orders in April.

Tuesday: The Fed meets Tuesday and Wednesday. Rates matter to this industry through equipment financing and warehouse cap rates more than through demand.

Wednesday: The ocean carriers' April 1 general rate increase announcements are due by mid-week under the FMC's 30-day notice rule. Count how many carriers file the full $300 and how many file $150.

Thursday: Existing home sales, which drive the appliance, furniture and building-materials freight that fills a lot of dry vans. Also the quarterly earnings of the largest US LTL carrier, whose tonnage commentary is the best real-time read on industrial demand we have.

Friday: Quarterly options expiry, which has nothing to do with freight, and the Port of Oakland's first Saturday gate the next day, which does.

Also: a big robotics trade show in Chicago all week; a Coast Guard hearing on the Baltimore bridge rebuild timeline; and the first stone-fruit shipments of the season out of the southern Central Valley, two weeks earlier than average.

See you tomorrow at 6.

